TAG bill lobbying effort now centered on Byrne

By NANCY GREENBERG - Senior Writer

	The lobbying effort by University students and officials will switch to Gov. Brendan Byrne's office, as the state Senate last week unanimously passed an amended Tuition Aid Grant (TAG) bill, considered to be more equitable to Rutgers and New Jersey's other four-year public institutions.
	The bill, passed in the Senate by a vote of 34-0, will move on to the governor's desk, where "there is some question as to whether or not the governor will approve the bill as is, reject it or limit the appropriation" from the state Treasury, said Arnold Zucker, University director of state relations.
	"The funds are absolutely essential, and we are hopeful they will remain," Zucker added.
	The original bill, which easily passed the Senate in September, was introduced to cover a $3.4 million deficit in a state student assistance program. The budget gap was caused by underestimations on the part of the state Department of Higher Education (DHE) regarding the effects of an economic downturn and the increased eligibility for TAG awards by resident students, resulting in more students than expected who accepted the offered aid.
	Before being amended, the bill called for Rutgers to pay between $1.2 and $1.6 million; the New Jersey Institute of Technology and the eight state colleges, between $1.4 and $1.8 million; and the DHE, kicking in the remaining $40,000.
	Representatives of the state's four-year schools termed the bill unfair because they had accepted tuition increases for the 1980-81 academic year to help offset the impact of inflation and maintain educational quality and would be financing the outcome of independent (private and community colleges) overspending their TAG allotments.
	Statewide student protests and opposition to the plan by faculty and administrators prompted the bill's sponsor, Assemblyman Albert Burnstein (D-Bergen) to amend the bill, with the Assembly approving the new legislation 60-4 earlier this month.
	In an apparent effort to take action on a series of delayed bills, the Senate passed the amended version requiring the University to pay $633,000 from the revenue generated from this year's tuition increase, a figure comparable to the amount of aid University students are receiving from the TAG program.
	State institutions of higher education will have to contribute a total of $1.4 million; the DHE, $60,000; private institutions receiving TAG money, $100,000; and the state general appropriations fund will provide about $1.3 million.
	Zucker said some of the student leaders were "extremely helpful and active. They are to be most commended."
	Student leaders said they will lobby the governor and his aides, Sen. Charles Yates (D-Burlington), who chairs the legislature's Joint Appropriations Committee, and Senate President Joseph Merlino (D-Mercer).
	"The amended bill keeps the TAG program alive, but spreads the burden of financing the deficit," said Rutgers College Governing Association President Tim Peterson, who attended the Senate session, along with sophomore Congressmen Todd Baker and Lynn Apuso.
	But Baker and Paul Bangiola, Livingston College Congress speaker, said the governor might veto the bill because the supplementary appropriation of $1.3 million is unavailable.
	The amended bill is "the best we can get," said Susan Kozel, student representative to the University's board of trustees. The Livingston senior, who today will send a letter to the governor and his aides, said the administration "hasn't communicated yet with students as to where their (stu-dents') energies would be most beneficial."
	Patrick Alwell, student representative to the board of governors said the Rutgers com- munity should be glad a more equitable bill was passed, but he hopes "the governor won't knock what progress we've made with the sweep of a pen."
